[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-7249"] :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-27528"] :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/b23108c36c016f3022dd3650bbd8bb70ebea27c19f8b8f62d439a4eebe7187f4.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/3dcd10cdeec52258b04705865196a1d59269c298b3821b87f7b0afe7727f740b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-28981/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "6f7ef81e-2059-543d-af83-dfffa4b44570" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28981/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "725afeb6-e3bb-5ca4-940a-eac455458a9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28981/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "14888b4c-a03a-5a91-b79f-1edee3a59218" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28981/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c8c363ef-2f3c-5f67-88ba-30824c194a81" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28981/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f8b5e499-e673-54e4-81cf-24244582d966" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28981/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe47b545-d0c9-53dd-a1b1-09cea81f1901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-28981/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-28981/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-28981/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-28981/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-28981/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-28981/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-28981" :question/id #uuid "dec52dca-c6e8-5d10-98b6-f6a6bc404648" :question/math-academy-id "q-28981" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\n- Its range is $y>0$\n- It is an increasing function\n- $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-28981/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-28981"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-47745/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "cee47831-efc3-5955-9a5d-9a0763761256" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47745/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "88323acb-113b-5d92-9851-0de21f1a8446" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47745/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "2370c36c-b2b6-52ac-ba2f-905cea931fdd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47745/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8f280868-28b0-5807-a0bc-bf255d95d371" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47745/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "aaff22bf-6cfd-56c9-b196-6794367840c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47745/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "56aa4da9-c9b0-598b-92b5-ccea76c3b639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-47745/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-47745/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-47745/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-47745/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-47745/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-47745/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-47745" :question/id #uuid "25c049b0-d191-57a8-a5d5-4a1829c58e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47745" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}?$\n\n- Its domain is $x>0$\n- Its range is $-\\infty <y<\\infty $\n- It has a horizontal asymptote $y=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=(0.125)^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/20fae3a813e249a6423567b448a1f5515c3b9127e7c0b82aad541e720db03aca.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $y=0$ is a horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-47745/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-47745"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167338/selection/5be1ff170b53a1d989c734612bf7d0208ac56c763f8ddad8ac31b5c20e95a0e5" :answer/id #uuid "fd61aa77-1866-549b-81ab-e873fa601dbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{28}{5}"