455cdfa0072deb754391feb4d916082568c248d95ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,7)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167338/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167338"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
